  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Pre-Assessment",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pre-Assessment",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does it mean for a web app to respond to a user?**\n\nStudents get their first encounter with the DOM as the live, programmable representation of a webpage. They distinguish events from elements, see how JavaScript connects user actions to DOM updates, and meet accessibility through page structure — screen readers use heading hierarchy as a navigation map. The lesson lands the mental model that drives the rest of the unit: interactivity = user action triggers a JavaScript event that changes the DOM.",
        "preparation": "- Provide copies of the AI and Algorithmic Unit Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide copies of the Interactivity in Web Apps activity guide (one per pair)",
        "student_overview": "**What does it mean for a web app to respond to a user?**\n\nApps respond when you tap, swipe, and click — but how? This lesson opens the hood on the Document Object Model (DOM), the system that lets JavaScript change what's on the page. You spot the difference between actions (events) and parts of the page (elements), watch a single click change what you see, and start noticing who else might be using the page besides you."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can we use JavaScript to make our websites more interactive?**\n\nStudents write their first event handler. A kinesthetic role-play — Element, User, Listener, Handler — grounds the vocabulary before the JavaScript appears on screen. This is also the unit's first explicit AI literacy moment: \"AI Confidence ≠ AI Correctness\" and \"Context Blindness\" are named, with a Show / Describe / Confirm debugging protocol when AI gives students code that doesn't fit their page. Front-End Developer enters as a real role through Maya's day-in-the-life chatbot interview.",
        "student_overview": "**How can we use JavaScript to make our websites more interactive?**\n\nA button isn't just a button — something is listening for your click, and something else handles what happens next. You learn the difference between an event listener and an event handler, write your first JavaScript that makes a page respond, and meet Maya, a Front-End Developer who builds this kind of thing every day. You also see your first moment of an AI giving you confident, wrong code — and learn how to catch it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can I make my app “remember” what the user did and show it on screen?**\n\nStudents meet variables as the way apps \"remember\" state and learn the create-update-display pattern. The lesson centers QA Engineer as a role and introduces a bug-triage workflow: severity, expected vs. actual, hypothesis-first. The three Evaluate AI patterns — Is the output what you asked for? Does it work? Can you explain it? — give students shared vocabulary for critiquing AI suggestions instead of just accepting them.",
        "preparation": "- Prepare blank sheets of paper for students to use in the changing values simulation activity\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app “remember” what the user did and show it on screen?**\n\nApps remember things — your score, your cart, your scroll position — using variables. You learn how to create them, update them, and display them on the page. You also step into the shoes of a QA Engineer: the person whose job is to find bugs before users do. By the end of class, you know how to write a clean bug report and how to tell whether AI's \"fix\" actually fixes anything."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nStudents learn arrow functions, named vs. anonymous syntax, and abstraction — with the framing that clean, well-named code helps humans and AI work with shared code. The spec.md activity introduces literal [Specify] and [Predict] tags, the unit's first explicit handoff from natural-language planning to AI-generated code. \"Write Rewind on your own\" is the unit's first declared Write First task — AI Tutor will not write it.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nRepeating the same lines of code over and over gets messy fast. Functions let you write a chunk of code once and reuse it everywhere. You learn to define and call arrow functions, why naming matters (for you, your teammates, and the AI tools you work with), and how to write a quick plan in `spec.md` before you ask AI to generate code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nStudents meet flowcharts and conditional statements together. The lesson names computer vision as the AI capability that lets students hand a sketched flowchart to AI as a visual spec — connecting visual planning directly to code generation. The Warm Up plants a Domain 4 hook (\"Who decided that liking one video means showing similar ones?\"), opening a conversation that returns in later lessons.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nApps make decisions all day — show this if logged in, otherwise show that. The tool for planning those decisions is a flowchart, and the code that runs them is the `if/else` statement. You sketch your first flowchart, hand it to AI as a visual spec, and check whether the code AI writes actually follows your plan. You also start asking a bigger question: who decided the rules the apps you use follow?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: Functions with Parameters",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Functions with Parameters",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make functions more flexible by passing information into them?**\n\nParameters come alive through the \"Guess the Pizza\" kinesthetic activity — one function, many pizzas. The spec.md plan-first pattern repeats, this time with parameter planning explicit in the spec. The lesson treats parameters as the way one function adapts to many use cases, planting the seed that parameterization is how features serve different users at scale.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Print copies of the Functions with Parameters activity guide if your students will not use Sketch Lab",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make functions more flexible by passing information into them?**\n\nA pizza app needs to take orders for any pizza — not just one. That's where parameters come in: they let a function take in information and adapt. You'll play \"Guess the Pizza\" with a partner, then learn to write functions that take parameters and use a plan in `spec.md` to tell AI what your function should take in and what it should return."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: Functions with Parameters",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 7: Building More Dynamic Pages",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Building More Dynamic Pages",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app make more dramatic changes to the page?**\n\nThis is the synthesis lesson for Phase 1. Students get the Event → State → Logic → Update mental model and apply POUR (Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust) to their own interactive features. Level 3 asks students to plan, build, label, evaluate, and improve a dynamic feature — the unit's first labeling exercise, which previews the Claim step of SPEC. Accessibility becomes a design responsibility here, not an afterthought.",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app make more dramatic changes to the page?**\n\nYou've learned events, variables, conditionals, and functions — now you put them together to build a feature that does something dramatic on the page. You see how every interaction breaks down into Event → State → Logic → Update. You also apply POUR — a four-letter way to check that your feature works for everyone, not just users like you — and label the parts of your code you actually understand."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 7: Building More Dynamic Pages",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 8: Nested Conditionals for Complex Logic",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Nested Conditionals for Complex Logic",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app handle multiple layers of decisions?**\n\nNested conditionals get their first formal treatment, paired with a test-all-4-outcomes discipline (TT / TF / FT / FF) anchored in the Movie Spoiler Checker. The lesson explicitly connects branching code to how AI systems classify — and the discussion goal flags moments where students notice an AI implementation has oversimplified or encoded bias. This is the unit's most direct bridge between code logic and algorithmic decision-making.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Nested Conditionals activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app handle multiple layers of decisions?**\n\nSometimes one yes/no question isn't enough — your app needs to check one thing, then another. That's a nested conditional, and it's how apps decide whether to show a spoiler alert, age-gate a video, or send you a recommendation. You plan a nested decision with a flowchart, test every possible outcome (there are always four), and think about what AI assumes when it writes branching code for you."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 8: Nested Conditionals for Complex Logic",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can my app check more than one condition at the same time?**\n\nThe lesson opens with pattern-discovery — checkboxes that fill in truth tables — before formally naming `&&` and `||`. The flowchart short-circuit demo makes evaluation order visible: when the first condition is false, the second one isn't even checked. Multi-condition rules become the lever for asking who an app's logic includes and excludes, setting up the deeper fairness work in the next lesson.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Combining Conditions with AND/OR activity guide (one for each student)\n- (Optional) Print copies of the unplugged Creating a Multi-Decision Flowchart activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can my app check more than one condition at the same time?**\n\nApps often need to check more than one thing at once. \"Free shipping if your order is over $50 AND you're a member.\" \"Free movie ticket if you're under 12 OR over 65.\" You fill in truth tables by toggling checkboxes, then write your own AND/OR rules in code. Along the way, you start asking — whose interest does each rule serve?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Combining Conditions with AND/OR",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can I design and debug more complex conditional rules?**\n\nNOT (`!`) is introduced through UI toggle states — mute, dark mode, password visibility — so students see \"not on\" as a useful condition, not an abstraction. The lesson combines `!`, `&&`, and `||` into complex Boolean expressions and asks students to refactor nested conditionals into cleaner combined ones. The \"AI says: Not eligible\" Warm Up and the \"Who might be excluded?\" Check for Understanding make fairness and inclusivity explicit design considerations.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I design and debug more complex conditional rules?**\n\nSometimes you need to do the opposite — show this if a setting is NOT on, allow access if you're NOT a guest. The NOT operator (`!`) gives you that. You'll combine NOT with AND and OR to write complex rules, refactor messy nested conditions into cleaner ones, and pause to ask: when AI says \"Not eligible,\" what's the rule it's using — and who does that rule leave out?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
      "name": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I design and build an interactive web app that responds to users in meaningful ways?**\n\nThe unit capstone — five days, four milestones, one student-designed interactive app. Students plan with a wireframe and flowchart, build, test, peer-review (TAG: Tell / Ask / Give), and finalize. Reflection prompts at each milestone push students to distinguish what AI did from what they did, building toward the Claim step they'll formalize in later units. Every Phase 1 concept comes together here: event-driven design, variables, functions, and conditionals including combined conditions.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Reactive Logic Project Planning Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I design and build an interactive web app that responds to users in meaningful ways?**\n\nFive days. One app. Your idea, your plan, your code. You use everything you've learned this unit — events, variables, functions, if/else, AND/OR, NOT — to build an interactive app that responds to the user. You plan it, build it in code, get feedback from your peers, and reflect on what worked, what didn't, and where AI helped vs. where you figured it out yourself. By the end, you have something to show — and to keep."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "ai-and-algorithmic-decisions-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 13,
