  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "cc4be6f5-cbac-4770-8dcd-254a40f546f2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc4be6f5-cbac-4770-8dcd-254a40f546f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cfe99ff7-5f41-4171-8309-d29d4bbea69c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b64ed37-905f-4e58-8bf0-581053ae0cec",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students distinguish between actions (things happening) and elements (parts of a webpage) as a foundation for understanding interactivity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which of these do you think are actions? Which are parts of the page?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should differentiate between actions and parts of a web page to build a foundation for interactivity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that all the items identified as actions are called events, and all the parts of the page are elements. Connect this back to what they just sorted so it feels like naming their ideas, not introducing new content.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is one action you wish your previous project could have done?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that their previous projects included parts of a webpage but did not respond to user actions. They begin to distinguish between an action (event) and what the page does in response, setting up the need for interactivity.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may describe outcomes (\"show a message\") instead of events (\"click a button\"). If needed, guide them to name the action first, then what the page should do in response."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b64ed37-905f-4e58-8bf0-581053ae0cec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1028bde2-3cc3-4614-901b-88ccfa90f131",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1028bde2-3cc3-4614-901b-88ccfa90f131",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a89df17-0660-41ef-a053-967ebf898285",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Last unit you designed web pages, think about the pages you built. Your pages had elements, but could they respond to events or do any of the things we just listed? In the previous unit, you focused on designing what a page looks like. In this unit, you learn how to make your pages interactive.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a89df17-0660-41ef-a053-967ebf898285",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f5f4b542-2902-4122-99c3-9dea31285452",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f5f4b542-2902-4122-99c3-9dea31285452",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ef619681-9b28-4dec-99c7-280d53e778fe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f13059d-b781-4250-beba-17c2f7597961",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand the DOM is what allows parts of the page to be changed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide and the Interactivity in Web Apps activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to walk through the annotated web page and point out specific parts such as the button, text, image, and input. Remind students that these are the parts they identified earlier in the warm-up. Briefly point to the diagram on the right to show that these parts can be grouped and organized within the page, but don't go into detail about the structure. Explain that when something happens on a page, like a click or typing, that is an event, and events happen to these parts. Emphasize that when events happen, these parts of the page can change.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "DOM Introduction",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the structure diagram and assume they need to understand how everything is organized. Keep the focus on the idea that a webpage is made of parts that can change. Treat the diagram as a quick visual reference, not something students need to analyze in detail."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f13059d-b781-4250-beba-17c2f7597961",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42f9ee56-6376-4ea9-a7e4-ac6c44fab84d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42f9ee56-6376-4ea9-a7e4-ac6c44fab84d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bce85a2-c30d-4ecb-8865-a89dc7e40cdb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Document Object Model (DOM)*. Note that something (code) has to tell the page what to change, and that they will look at what causes those changes next.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the term \"object\" or get confused by technical language. Keep the explanation grounded in what they can see: \"The DOM is what lets the page change\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bce85a2-c30d-4ecb-8865-a89dc7e40cdb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b404c8a-a563-4681-af85-ade07c1461fc",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b404c8a-a563-4681-af85-ade07c1461fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7543a8bc-a0b5-4fbb-9796-3807cd8297e7",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly explain that the structural parts students just identified — headings, buttons, images, navigation — do double duty. They organize the page visually for sighted users, and they help screen reader users navigate the page without seeing it. Emphasize that a heading is not just styled text; it functions as a landmark that screen readers can jump to."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7543a8bc-a0b5-4fbb-9796-3807cd8297e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1a438ec-da00-472d-a5e5-2c269c83c553",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you build a webpage, you're not just deciding what it looks like. You're deciding how someone gets around it. Sighted users scroll and scan. Screen reader users jump from heading to heading, or button to button, to figure out what's on a page. If your headings are just big text, a screen reader user has no map. If they're real headings, the page has a structure they can navigate.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1a438ec-da00-472d-a5e5-2c269c83c553",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "badcdd45-683e-457e-8992-65cc6bab865e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that when something changes on a webpage, it doesn't happen automatically—there is code that tells the page what to do. That code is called JavaScript. Explain that JavaScript listens for user actions (events) and tells the page how to respond. Let students know this is the language they will use to make their web apps interactive.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think the user action itself (\"I clicked\") causes the change. Reinforce that something in the page is \"watching\" for that action and telling the page what to do. Keep the explanation simple; focus on the idea that JavaScript is what makes the page respond, not how the code works."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "badcdd45-683e-457e-8992-65cc6bab865e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b50241f-4727-4321-ae92-fea7e1df5dad",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b50241f-4727-4321-ae92-fea7e1df5dad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99a96a18-a656-46b2-90fd-855b11b59392",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain how interactivity is implemented. Ask students what they notice changed, then focus on the middle steps to explain how the change happens; emphasizing that JavaScript is the middle step, connecting the user action to what changes on the page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1. Explain that the page has many parts, but it does not respond to events yet. Students explore the page and identify its parts in their activity guide, then decide which parts could change. Students choose one interaction to make work and use AI Tutor to make that change happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who can identify multiple parts of the page and clearly describe what they want to change. Ask: \"What part are you focusing on\" or \"What do you want the page to do?\" or \"What happens right now when you select a button?\"",
        "progression_name": "Static vs Interactive Web App",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may try to make multiple parts work at once. Encourage them to focus on one interaction and get it working first. If students are stuck, prompt them to describe what the user does and what should change on the page before using AI."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99a96a18-a656-46b2-90fd-855b11b59392",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b5dcf64-bbb3-489c-b858-fbea58632b77",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b5dcf64-bbb3-489c-b858-fbea58632b77",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63fd8ea5-9072-4374-99a6-9f661431ffd9",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What made the page change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students may say \"the button,\" \"clicking,\" or \"the AI.\" Guide students to recognize that the page changed because code made it respond. Introduce that this code is JavaScript, which allows the page to update. Emphasize that these updates happen in the DOM, which is what allows parts of the page to change.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the action (\"I clicked\") or the tool (\"the AI\") instead of the underlying cause. Acknowledge their responses, then help them connect the idea that code made the page respond. Avoid going into detail about how the code works—focus on the idea that JavaScript enables the change."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63fd8ea5-9072-4374-99a6-9f661431ffd9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7ad90c9-6ff2-4dc5-a043-3ca54511c364",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7ad90c9-6ff2-4dc5-a043-3ca54511c364",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72ed03c8-3756-42fe-89fd-a1cba6ee3071",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that interactivity affects user experience and that some interactions work better than others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly explain that interactivity means a page responds when a user does something, like clicking, typing, or other events. Keep the explanation short and grounded in examples students have already experienced.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Trade-Offs in Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72ed03c8-3756-42fe-89fd-a1cba6ee3071",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dcc8f5f6-dc0a-465b-a017-76a7a16169a3",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just made a page interactive. But here's the next question . . . does every interaction automatically make a page better? Let's test that. You're going to try two versions of an app that both have interaction and compare how easy they are to use.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume that \"more interaction = better.\" Do not correct this immediately. Let students test this idea in the next activity so they can experience the difference themselves."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dcc8f5f6-dc0a-465b-a017-76a7a16169a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6b0ae4b-adcc-4c4c-acfb-2dc862b5f726",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6b0ae4b-adcc-4c4c-acfb-2dc862b5f726",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1bee3a0-a8e2-41ad-8ce6-853801f34e47",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 5.\n- Level 2: Students read about interactivity in web apps and accessibility.\n- Level 3: Students experience an app that has a lot of frustrating interactivity.\n- Level 4: Students experience an app of the same purpose that has a much better user experience.\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Emphasize that their goal is to compare the two experiences and think about how the design affects the user. Look for students who are able to describe what is frustrating in Level 2 and what works better in Level 3. Ask: \"What made this version harder to use\", \"What made the other version easier?\", or \"What changed for the user?\"",
        "progression_name": "Document Design Flaws"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1bee3a0-a8e2-41ad-8ce6-853801f34e47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd108cc1-aca5-44de-8182-4bb3a295341c",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on surface features (colors, layout) instead of interaction. Guide them to focus on what happens when they click, type, or try to complete a task. Encourage students to complete the task in each app rather than just clicking around so they fully experience the frustration or improvement."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd108cc1-aca5-44de-8182-4bb3a295341c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0ddeed8-4adb-4220-aec6-9ead94e27596",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0ddeed8-4adb-4220-aec6-9ead94e27596",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac30756c-c954-4444-aa84-e3b0add14a12",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which version was better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that not all interactions improve the user experience and begin to connect interaction to design. Guide students to understand that interactivity is not just about making things happen, but about making things happen in a way that helps the user.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may give general answers like \"this one was better\" without explaining why. Prompt them to describe specific interactions (what happened when they clicked or typed) and how those interactions affected their experience. If students focus only on visual appearance, redirect them to how the app responded to their actions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac30756c-c954-4444-aa84-e3b0add14a12",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e9c948b-b851-4cf9-ab4c-749cc96565e7",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e9c948b-b851-4cf9-ab4c-749cc96565e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "793af7e9-fe1f-4b34-b9f1-fb1947288a84",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Quick Design Sprint section of their activity guide. Ensure they choose a specific user to focus on. Students can either draw the app and label their improvements or write a short description of how they would improve it for that user. If students are unsure who to choose, provide examples such as someone using it quickly, someone who gets confused easily, or someone using it on a small screen.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may suggest changes that are not connected to a specific user (e.g., \"make it better\" or \"add more features\"). Prompt them to explain who their change is helping and why. If needed, ask: \"How does this help your user?\" or \"What problem are you solving for them?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "793af7e9-fe1f-4b34-b9f1-fb1947288a84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3c79c5e-9a06-4283-9eaa-4dd40f0875da",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3c79c5e-9a06-4283-9eaa-4dd40f0875da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01e5f7b0-0745-4873-8bf1-8bb15f291ea2",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Would your change make anything harder for someone else?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that improving an app for one user can sometimes create challenges for others. Guide students to notice that design decisions often involve trade-offs, where making something better in one way may make it harder in another.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume their change is only beneficial and have difficulty identifying downsides. Prompt them to think about different users by asking, \"Who might find this harder to use?\" or \"What could be confusing about this change?\" If needed, provide examples to model how one improvement can create a new challenge."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01e5f7b0-0745-4873-8bf1-8bb15f291ea2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6c4121fd-6236-4719-b54b-f4f986f1d4f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59287f99-92c1-45f3-bdb2-9db954ab5cd6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how a web app responds to a user by describing an event and what changes on the page. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59287f99-92c1-45f3-bdb2-9db954ab5cd6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1006605d-ea06-4cbf-a929-dc19cedd3edd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6134e996-539e-4638-a1e5-cfca08ea9cac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect the role of a front-end developer to building interactivity on a webpage and recognize JavaScript as the tool that makes interactions happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> Do This: Briefly spotlight the role of a Front-End Developer. Explain that this role is responsible for what users see and how a webpage responds when they interact with it.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Invite students to name a website or app where something responds instantly (e.g., a button click, animation, or form). Ask, \"What do you think the developer had to decide to make that interaction work?\" This helps students connect the role to real interactions they use every day."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6134e996-539e-4638-a1e5-cfca08ea9cac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5294e91d-611c-4042-a8e7-7ad8d2fab596"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2d86d27-4681-40dc-90de-8af2982584ac",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2d86d27-4681-40dc-90de-8af2982584ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5294e91d-611c-4042-a8e7-7ad8d2fab596"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71e6bb77-76a6-4ea5-99a6-65e768706748",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *A front-end developer uses JavaScript to make a button respond to a click. If that developer used AI to build a “Like” button, what would they need to tell the AI to make it work?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that a developer must clearly describe both the user action and what should happen in response when using AI to build an interaction. They begin to notice that something must detect the action and trigger the response, rather than the change happening automatically. This sets up the idea that interactions follow a structured process, which will be explored through event listeners and event handlers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71e6bb77-76a6-4ea5-99a6-65e768706748",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5294e91d-611c-4042-a8e7-7ad8d2fab596"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75b25e72-03f8-4dd0-9f8b-30ba12583a18",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand how webpages respond to user actions by connecting the roles of event listener (detect) and event handler (respond).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *event listener*. Emphasize that it is attached to an element and waits for a specific user action (click, hover, key press).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *event handler*. Emphasize that this is the response — what actually happens after the event.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students quickly form small groups of three to four and assign one role per person: Element, User, Listener, and Handler. Emphasize that each role represents a part of what happens behind the scenes when someone interacts with a web page. Give students about 30–60 seconds to decide who will take on each role, and prompt them to read their role name aloud within their group so everyone is clear.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups assign roles, circulate to ensure each group has all roles covered and to clarify confusion, especially between the Event Listener and Event Handler. If needed, briefly restate that the listener waits for the action and the handler decides what happens next.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each group decide on a simple interaction such as a tap, clap, or press. Once they've chosen their interaction, direct them to say the three planning statements out loud together within their group: what their event is, what the listener is waiting for, and what the handler will do. Emphasize that this is a quick call-and-response style step and should take no more than 1–2 minutes. The goal is for every group to clearly state their plan before acting, not to write anything down or overthink it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups plan, circulate to ensure each group has selected a clear interaction and is actually saying their responses out loud. If a group is quiet or vague, prompt them to say their plan together and be more specific about what will happen.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Intro to Event Handling",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage groups to keep interactions simple (tap, clap, press) so the focus stays on the sequence, not the scenario."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75b25e72-03f8-4dd0-9f8b-30ba12583a18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50b39c54-8544-4709-a0c9-7521130c7342",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50b39c54-8544-4709-a0c9-7521130c7342",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24b9e1b1-c7eb-4a98-a4f3-f8486e3bab17",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct them to act it out using the four-step sequence: the user performs the action, the listener announces that the event was detected and calls \"Run,\" and the handler performs the response. Emphasize that the handler cannot act until the listener calls it, reinforcing the idea that something must detect the event before code runs. Give groups a minute or two to run through the sequence at least once, and encourage them to repeat it with a different response if they finish quickly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students act out the sequence, circulate to ensure groups are following the correct order and using the language of \"event,\" \"listener,\" and \"handler\" appropriately. If a group skips a step or has the handler acting too early, pause them and ask which step might be missing. Pay close attention to confusion between the listener and handler roles, and reinforce that the listener detects and triggers while the handler performs the response.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make sure groups explicitly say \"Run\" before the handler acts. This step represents the moment code executes and is critical for understanding how event handling works.\n\nIf students confuse the listener and handler roles, restate that the listener detects and triggers, while the handler performs the action."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24b9e1b1-c7eb-4a98-a4f3-f8486e3bab17",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f46db61-3cea-4c23-9e9d-a95caa095736",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f46db61-3cea-4c23-9e9d-a95caa095736",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd00bc7e-5cac-4d4b-a157-a5aa0b6df523",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Who detected the event?*\n- *Who triggered the action?*\n- *Who performed the action?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that different parts of the system have distinct roles in making an interaction happen. The event listener is responsible for detecting when a user action occurs, but it does not perform the action itself—it triggers the next step. The event handler is what actually runs and produces the visible change on the page. Through this discussion, guide students to understand the sequence: a user action (event) is detected by a listener, which then calls the handler to perform the response. This reinforces that interactions do not happen automatically—there is a structured process that connects user input to changes on the screen, which will later map directly to how event listeners and event handlers work in code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd00bc7e-5cac-4d4b-a157-a5aa0b6df523",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d14b6ed4-667f-4b7e-9f2a-cee76a3e1c33",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will apply the Select → Listen → Respond model to build interactive features, use AI to generate event-based code, and verify that their code correctly targets elements and updates the page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain that what students just acted out is how event handling works on a web page:\n- The first step is interacts. The user interacts with an element, like clicking a button. Nothing changes yet — this is just the action the user takes.\n- The second step is listen. The page listens for that action. This is the event listener — it waits for a specific event, like a click, and detects when it happens.\n- The final step is respond. The event listener calls the event handler, and the event handler runs the code. This is where something actually changes on the page, like text updating or an image appearing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this code as representing the same three-step process students just acted out for how a web page responds to a user action.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that this line of code selects an element on the page (like the button).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that this line of code adds an event listener. It tells the page what event to listen for — so in this case, a click.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that this line of code is the event handler, which responds to the event. The code that runs and updates the page (text, image, color, etc.).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI-generated code may look correct but still not work, and model how to question and verify whether the code actually matches the event or selected element from the prompt. (especially IDs).",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Make a Button Respond"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d14b6ed4-667f-4b7e-9f2a-cee76a3e1c33",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e11acb22-4114-4c38-9a9e-e14f58395dad",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI sounds confident and can generate code that looks correct, but it doesn’t actually know if it will work on your specific page. It is making a best guess based on patterns, so when we see something like this ID \"buttonID,\" we have to stop and ask: does an element with this ID actually exist in our web page, because if it doesn't, nothing will happen even though the code looks right.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e11acb22-4114-4c38-9a9e-e14f58395dad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8cc3a08c-89c2-40c9-973d-48cd332b0ab4",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the term context blindness as the reason AI's code can look completely correct but still fail. Explain that AI generates code based on patterns it has seen in other pages, not on what is actually in the project. The ID it guesses might exist somewhere on the internet, just not in the project."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8cc3a08c-89c2-40c9-973d-48cd332b0ab4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9c7a13d-4367-446a-a054-7d261092c701",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When AI gives you code that doesn't work, it's not because AI is broken. It's because AI doesn't know what's in your page. It's guessing based on patterns from code it has seen before. That guess is usually plausible — it looks right — but plausible isn't the same as correct. This kind of mistake has a name: context blindness. AI is blind to your context. That's why showing AI your element, describing it in detail, and asking it to confirm matter so much. You're filling in the context AI can't see.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9c7a13d-4367-446a-a054-7d261092c701",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0e89d3c-8d48-4933-b7dc-dd35e91b591d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to introduce each steps students can use to create and debug AI-generated code. As each tip appears, connect it to how it helps improve their prompt:\n- First, show the element. Emphasize that including a screenshot helps AI understand what part of the page the student is working with.\n- Next, describe the element in detail. Highlight that students should include specific details like text, color, or position so AI can correctly identify it.\n- Finally, confirm the element. Model asking AI to verify which element it's selecting to make sure it's targeting the correct one."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0e89d3c-8d48-4933-b7dc-dd35e91b591d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5bdc7e00-58cc-4328-ac59-ffea2d586071",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When your button isn't working, don't just guess! Be specific. First, show the element so the AI can see what you're working with. Then describe the element in detail — what it says, what it looks like, where it is. And if it's still not working, ask AI to confirm which element it's selecting, because sometimes it's targeting the wrong one.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5bdc7e00-58cc-4328-ac59-ffea2d586071",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "828cda5a-d75c-4c5c-ba9c-61762db793b0",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you're going to apply what you learned by acting like a front-end developer—your job is to make a webpage respond to user actions by selecting elements, adding event listeners, and writing event handlers. You can use AI to help generate code, but you also need to test it and make sure it actually works when you interact with the page.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "828cda5a-d75c-4c5c-ba9c-61762db793b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af985f57-83f1-4ff8-9750-1313b58d1e40",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 2, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 6.\n- Level 1: Predict and trace what the code will do when a button is clicked.\n- Level 2: Use AI to generate code for one button interaction.\n- Level 3: Write code yourself to add a second button interaction.\n- Level 4: Conduct a day-in-the-life interview with a front-end developer chatbot.\n- Level 5: Build an interactive app based on a chosen scenario.\n- Level 6: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Level 1, focus on helping students connect the code to the event handling model. Check that they can identify where the code selects an element, listens for an event, and responds. Ask questions like, \"What event is this listening for?\" and \"What actually changes on the page when you click?\" In Levels 2 and 3, support students in working with AI-generated code. Watch for common issues like nothing happening on click or the wrong element responding. Prompt students with, \"Which element is this code targeting?\" and \"How can you tell if it's the right one?\" In Level 5, push students toward fully working interactions. Encourage them to test each button and verify that every interaction works as expected.",
        "progression_name": "Adding Interactivity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af985f57-83f1-4ff8-9750-1313b58d1e40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fae4120d-fa9c-4208-bde3-31048079fbe8",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to test frequently after adding each interaction. Remind them that if nothing happens, it's usually because the code is not targeting the correct element or the interaction isn't set up properly."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 1 through 5 to assess whether students can add an event handler to make a button interactive and update page content when a button is clicked. Look for evidence that students can correctly select elements, attach event listeners, and create responses that change text or visuals on the page."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fae4120d-fa9c-4208-bde3-31048079fbe8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "001a5923-8547-4c73-b24d-7a0de6f7263b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff662afa-887f-4a26-a1e4-9ad3fdee4654",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how a web page responds to a click by describing both what the user does and what changes on the page. Prompt students to be specific about what should happen when a button is clicked. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What part of being a Front-End Developer did you like?*\n- *What part was frustrating?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reflect on their experience of doing Front-End Developer work and start forming personal opinions about whether the role fits them. Listen for specific responses — what they liked or didn't like about the actual work, not just the activity. Students might mention the satisfaction of seeing something they built respond to a click, the creative freedom of picking content, the frustration of debugging AI-generated code, or the cognitive load of switching between writing prompts and reading code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff662afa-887f-4a26-a1e4-9ad3fdee4654",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e717c33a-741f-4880-b54c-8c83c651f2e6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec6f87f8-7f71-49c6-a9c6-4a7e0172af0b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine you're a front-end developer at YouTube, and your job is to decide what videos show up on the homepage. All you can see are the buttons people click and information they submit. Based on just those actions, you have to decide which videos to show millions of people.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec6f87f8-7f71-49c6-a9c6-4a7e0172af0b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b423cffb-c464-4638-bb45-dcda07ecf86e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5ca0757-6005-458b-bfb5-33bd5e56bc87",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What do you notice when users click, type, or post?*\n- *What changes each time someone interacts?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** \nStudents should identify the types of data YouTube tracks from user interactions (likes, views, comments, watch time, searches) and recognize that these values update over time. They should also identify the specific parts of the page (like/dislike buttons, play/pause controls, comment fields, search bars) that capture user actions and trigger those updates. Guide students to connect what they see on the screen to underlying variables that are stored and updated behind the scenes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What data is YouTube tracking when users interact with the site? What parts of the page collect that data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify the types of data YouTube might track from user interactions (likes, views, comments, watch time, searches) and understand that these values are stored as variables that continuously update over time and shape what users see on the platform. Help students bring in examples (scores, timers, progress bars) from other sites and guide them to connect what they see on the screen to underlying variables that are updated behind the scenes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5ca0757-6005-458b-bfb5-33bd5e56bc87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b423cffb-c464-4638-bb45-dcda07ecf86e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d50fd93d-f2ad-41a8-9ba8-fce2a930d12d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students experience how a value can change repeatedly while still being tracked — modeling the concept of a variable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "From User Actions to Variables"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d50fd93d-f2ad-41a8-9ba8-fce2a930d12d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e9fd0d4-cc7d-4d62-a6cf-f44cea1d36ae",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you click like, post a comment, or search on YouTube, the app has to keep track of what you did and update what you see. Programmers have specific names for the pieces that make that possible.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e9fd0d4-cc7d-4d62-a6cf-f44cea1d36ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9bf7357-8f00-4414-bafa-576d273e1178",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect back to the warm-up by reminding students that the like count, comment text, and search history they identified are all examples of these concepts in action.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As you move through the next three slides, refer back to specific examples students gave during the warm-up. For instance, if a student mentioned the like count going up, you can use that as your concrete example when defining variable (the like count is stored) and operation (clicking the like button adds 1)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9bf7357-8f00-4414-bafa-576d273e1178",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40ecf006-e876-4a25-bf4b-fc1997b65be7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40ecf006-e876-4a25-bf4b-fc1997b65be7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a88da953-ccfa-4c05-a7d9-354b31d37a52",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *variable*. Connect the definition back to the warm-up by reminding students of the YouTube examples they identified — the like count, the number of views, the comments, and the search history are all variables. They are containers that store information the app needs to keep track of. The value inside can change as users interact with the app.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck on the idea of a variable as a container, help them make the connection by explaining how you can take out what's inside and replace it with something new but you can only store one thing at a time. For now, students don't have to know the difference between let (a variable whose value can change) and const (a variable whose value is fixed) but they should recognize that those keywords indicate creating a new variable."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a88da953-ccfa-4c05-a7d9-354b31d37a52",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01c23ba6-f192-4263-ab64-60251eac039d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01c23ba6-f192-4263-ab64-60251eac039d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e64edb8-3b4c-4f7f-8b67-b51eac685550",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *operation*. Connect this concept back to the warm-up by explaining that every time someone clicked the like button on YouTube, the like count went up by one. That action — adding one to the like count — is an operation. In code, operations are what make variables change in response to user actions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e64edb8-3b4c-4f7f-8b67-b51eac685550",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9deb2ae6-494e-4bcf-869f-fd3bdd1cc52e",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This three-step pattern — create, update, display — is the foundation for almost every interactive web app. Whenever you see a number or piece of text update on a page, this kind of code is running behind the scenes.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9deb2ae6-494e-4bcf-869f-fd3bdd1cc52e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8233c98a-be85-463a-bb53-3a95fcf75c67",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the three lines of code one at a time. Explain that this is the pattern students will use and see over and over again: create a variable, update its value, and display the value on the page.\n- Line 1 creates the variable score and sets its starting value to 0. This is the program's starting state.\n- Line 2 updates the value stored in score. The variable name appears on both sides of the equals sign because the program needs to know which variable to update and what the new value should be.\n- Line 3 shows the current value of score on the page so the user can see it.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may wonder why score = score + 1 looks strange compared to math class, where this would be a contradiction. Explain that in code, the equals sign means \"store the value on the right into the variable on the left\" — it's not a math equation. The program calculates score + 1 first, then stores the result back into score."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8233c98a-be85-463a-bb53-3a95fcf75c67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c4ea902-9077-4c96-9eee-1e3608a3fb85",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c4ea902-9077-4c96-9eee-1e3608a3fb85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91db4d87-a642-401c-9d8d-361fbcdce79f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce `let` and `const` as the two keywords used to create variables in JavaScript. Use `let` when the value will change while the program runs (like a score that goes up). Use `const` when the value should stay the same (like the maximum number of lives in a game)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91db4d87-a642-401c-9d8d-361fbcdce79f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ce86326-07c2-46cf-860b-4ab736b7c4d4",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you see let or const in code, that's a signal that a new variable is being created. The keyword tells you whether the value is meant to change.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students don't need to memorize a strict rule for when to use each keyword yet. The main takeaway is recognition: when they see let or const, they should know a variable is being created."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ce86326-07c2-46cf-860b-4ab736b7c4d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3f3b236-4c09-4635-8302-9336958f2128",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3f3b236-4c09-4635-8302-9336958f2128",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "838f78a2-d456-4f33-a937-0a4284054c0a",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore a dashboard in Sketch Lab and then in Weblab to observe how the score (state) changes over time. Then students generate and write code to track and update state through variables and user interactions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the role of QA Engineer. Emphasize that QA Engineers don't fix bugs — they find them, document them clearly enough that someone else can fix them, and decide which bugs are urgent enough to fix first.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *A bug report is a QA Engineer's main deliverable. What information did the QA Engineer include?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that a bug report contains specific kinds of information for specific reasons. Listen for them naming the parts: steps to reproduce (so the developer can see the bug themselves), expected vs. actual (so the developer knows what's wrong, not just that something is wrong), severity (so the team knows how urgent it is), and hypothesis (a useful starting guess, though the developer may find something different). If students focus on surface details like formatting, redirect them to function: \"Why does the developer need each piece?\"",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Working with Variables",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The hypothesis line is the most interesting part for students to notice. A QA Engineer doesn't just say \"it's broken\" — they form a guess about why. That guess might be wrong, but it gives the developer a starting point. This is the same kind of thinking students will do in the next level when they triage bugs."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "838f78a2-d456-4f33-a937-0a4284054c0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "295eea05-755f-4ac3-9e50-93d08cf1a37b",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "295eea05-755f-4ac3-9e50-93d08cf1a37b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2c3960d-a997-446f-9012-ecd02f0314f4",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Students explore how variables are set and change.\n- Level 2: Students choose one of the levels and use AI to debug code that should change a variable.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students explaining what value they are tracking and how it changes. Ask them what caused the variable to change and what the new value is. Have them confirm whether the result matched what they expected.",
        "progression_name": "Working with Variables"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2c3960d-a997-446f-9012-ecd02f0314f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9904b195-649c-4156-92fb-f6bec3138448",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students accept AI-generated code quickly, pause them to explain how the value changes before moving on. Emphasize that they must verify that the value updates correctly on the screen."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 to check whether students can:\n- define what the app should do before using AI\n- update a variable correctly\n- explain how the value (state) changes over time\n- compare expected vs actual behavior\n- use AI to generate, test, and verify solutions"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9904b195-649c-4156-92fb-f6bec3138448",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b5824b4-dc40-4286-8e7d-fcb10723ab4d",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b5824b4-dc40-4286-8e7d-fcb10723ab4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42cfdcbb-2719-40c6-a9bf-3f0674e18c8b",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students state the goal out loud (\"It should say Hello Sonia\"), then click through the animated slide to show what the AI actually produced. Emphasize the habit: always check if the AI gave you what you intended, not just whether it wrote code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students read the prompt and identify what the code is supposed to do (\"add 10 to score\"). Then have them trace through the code line by line and figure out what it actually does (\"subtracts 10, ending with -5\"). Emphasize the habit: read AI code carefully and compare it to what you asked for. Code that looks reasonable at a glance can still do the wrong thing.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that there's nothing visibly broken about this code — no typos, no missing pieces, no error-looking syntax. That's exactly why this habit matters. The only way to catch a bug like this is to read the code and compare it to the original goal."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42cfdcbb-2719-40c6-a9bf-3f0674e18c8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ce56a58-9a5b-43c6-8d3e-c591debab407",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ce56a58-9a5b-43c6-8d3e-c591debab407",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b7f78d0-4bc7-4bfa-bed1-1f33200ff0eb",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students trace through the code line by line and explain what is happening at each step. What is num after line 1? What does num + num evaluate to? What gets stored back in num after line 2? Emphasize the habit: even when AI code works correctly, you should be able to explain what it is doing. If you can't explain it, you don't really understand it — and you won't be able to fix it when something goes wrong.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This code is correct, which makes it different from the previous two examples. Students might be tempted to skim past it because there's no bug to catch. Push them to articulate why it works. A useful prompt: \"If a classmate asked you what num + num does, how would you explain it?\" Being able to put code into your own words is the difference between copying AI output and understanding it."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b7f78d0-4bc7-4bfa-bed1-1f33200ff0eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "488e2532-0bb4-4bc9-aa28-252e7130d671"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3766d96-c5e4-4aa9-80f4-20e8d5214579",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students give specific examples from their debugged code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What surprised you about this work?*\n- *What's different about being the person who finds problems vs. the person who builds features?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reflect on what it was like to do QA Engineer work and start comparing it to the Front-End Developer work they did last lesson. Listen for specific responses about the experience of finding problems — the patience required to test thoroughly, the satisfaction of catching something subtle, the frustration of bugs that don't reproduce, or the cognitive shift from \"make it work\" to \"find what's broken.\" For the second question, guide students to notice that the two roles require different mindsets: builders look for what to add, testers look for what's missing or wrong. Builders often want their code to work; testers need to want to break it. Neither role is better — they're complementary, and many software teams need both.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3766d96-c5e4-4aa9-80f4-20e8d5214579",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6140da2-7b34-4300-a4e1-edcbbfa1512a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f993ff4-af9e-469f-bc8e-c448a78e5f2a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize problems caused by repeated code and begin to identify qualities of clean, maintainable code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What problems might happen if code is repeated many times?*\n- *What would make code easier to work with?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that repeating code can lead to bugs, make updates harder, and slow developers down. They should begin to identify that code is easier to work with when it is organized, readable, and reusable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain why clean, organized code matters in real-world development.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f993ff4-af9e-469f-bc8e-c448a78e5f2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91ad2132-9e19-4a1e-88be-ebe1bf492c84"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25c3f092-abc0-41d3-ad21-a32934e41b40",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand that code can be grouped into a single behavior and that functions are used to name and reuse that behavior.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4 Level 1. Students make predictions, review the code, and make modifications to explore functions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students identify where the behavior is located. Check that they can distinguish between code that runs inside the event handler and behavior that could be moved into a function. Ask questions like: \"Where is the behavior right now?\" \"What is this code doing when it runs?\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introduction to Functions",
        "progression_name": "Exploring Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25c3f092-abc0-41d3-ad21-a32934e41b40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0186c42f-e92e-427a-b9e2-7be192ec0eb6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *function*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that this section of code tells JavaScript that we are creating a function and gives it a name. Recall the const keyword and explain that it is used to define a function because we don't want the value of the `addNotification` variable (which is the function) to change.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the empty parentheses and arrow (which is made using the equals sign and greater than symbol together) indicates this is a function. That makes this an arrow function.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that within the curly braces is the code we want to execute when the function is called.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that having a named function allows us to call it anywhere we want to use it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that there are two common ways to write arrow functions: named and anonymous. For named arrow functions, emphasize that because it has a name, it can be reused or called later in the code. For anonymous arrow function, clarify that they are often used \"in place,\" such as inside an event handler or passed into another function."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0186c42f-e92e-427a-b9e2-7be192ec0eb6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1739789-7853-4341-8604-227088bdd1c3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Think about it like this, if someone is anonymous, you can not call their name to get their attention. But if you know someone's name, you can call them anytime you need them. Named functions work the same way – we can call them again later.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1739789-7853-4341-8604-227088bdd1c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd74ad22-b37d-478f-80dd-f28c445e6fe5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand that abstraction hides details and that using named functions helps them organize and reuse behavior when wiring up interactions in an app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define abstraction. Emphasize that abstraction means hiding the details and focusing on what something does. Use the visual to compare what's happening inside versus using the function name, and highlight how the name helps us understand the behavior without seeing the code.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Writing Reusable Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd74ad22-b37d-478f-80dd-f28c445e6fe5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "938ad0d2-8d89-4651-baf7-375efcc61ac5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've already been doing these things — you predicted what buttons would do, you fixed AI's mistakes and explained why. Today we name the habit. SPEC is the four-letter framework you'll see all semester.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "938ad0d2-8d89-4651-baf7-375efcc61ac5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90d55fa1-ab80-4f52-9107-2cbed9588ea6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read each letter aloud. Tell students they'll see [Specify] and [Predict] tags inside the spec.md file in the next level.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which function is easier to understand without reading the code? Which one would AI be more confident about?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that clear function names improve understanding for both humans and AI, while vague names make it harder to predict what the code does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90d55fa1-ab80-4f52-9107-2cbed9588ea6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0eb578ca-8624-40b1-a562-bc59d01a724f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you're going to act like a developer building an interactive app. You'll look at an example, then use AI to help you generate functions for new buttons, connect them, and test that they work.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0eb578ca-8624-40b1-a562-bc59d01a724f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b9b1649-773a-48b1-b311-12bf3c66ad80",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- Level 2: Use AI to rewrite an event handler to use a function, then rewrite another event handler on their own.\n- Level 3: Use AI to implement functions to hook up buttons in a web app.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ensure they are following the spec → add named arrow function → add event listener → test process. Encourage students to test frequently after each change — when something doesn't work, have them check whether the function is defined correctly, whether it's being called, and whether the name matches what it does. Remind students that AI-generated code can look correct but still needs to be tested and verified.",
        "progression_name": "Write Reusable Functions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b9b1649-773a-48b1-b311-12bf3c66ad80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ff67193-8aa0-4851-a2eb-b839f9b601eb",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that AI is generating code based on their description. Clearer descriptions lead to better results. Encourage them to revise their prompt or ask AI to explain the code if something doesn't work."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 to assess whether students can meet the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Write arrow functions to organize code into reusable pieces\n- Refactor behavior into functions instead of writing it directly inside event handlers\n- Explain how abstraction makes code easier to maintain, including how naming and reusing functions improves clarity"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ff67193-8aa0-4851-a2eb-b839f9b601eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e2bbf-63e6-4311-bf44-0e7311fd9fd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "213784da-0972-41cd-af0d-1bd61fe3fe9b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students discuss their responses to the Question of the Day with a partner. After a few minutes, ask for volunteers to share with the class. Encourage students to use examples from their code today — such as how they renamed a function, created a reusable arrow function, or refactored repeated code — to explain how they made their code cleaner and easier to reuse.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give general answers (like \"I used functions\"), prompt them to be more specific by asking follow-up questions such as:\n- \"What change made your code easier for someone else to understand?\"\n- \"How might AI tools benefit from code that’s clearly named and organized?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "213784da-0972-41cd-af0d-1bd61fe3fe9b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2061c418-c526-4edd-b436-2549dd6e7b48",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2061c418-c526-4edd-b436-2549dd6e7b48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a342702f-aedc-4ed9-a947-d38fcc2189fc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a342702f-aedc-4ed9-a947-d38fcc2189fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4a39bedf-e0b9-4249-a2dc-a0a079a6e059"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87a1e7b2-be86-4303-8c22-5b84bff83d57",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What functionality of a social media app does this flowchart represent?*\n- *Where else have you seen an app make a decision like this?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that apps make decisions based on user input or data, similar to a flowchart. Use familiar examples like social media apps deciding what to show next or a login screen checking if a password is correct. Emphasize that apps are full of these decision points, and each decision affects what the user sees or experiences.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Who decided that liking one video means showing you similar ones?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that algorithmic systems reflect human decisions — someone designed the rules that shape what they see — and begin to see themselves as participants in (not just users of) those systems.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87a1e7b2-be86-4303-8c22-5b84bff83d57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e7d07585-293c-4cf2-be18-3ab6ccdb5f1e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c1c4ebd-88c9-4ddb-8061-b7a8095d9e6b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand the different flowchart components.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *flowchart*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the symbols that are often used to create flowcharts:\n- Start/End – where the process begins or stops\n- Process – an action the app or user takes\n- Decision – a question with Yes/No outcomes\n- Connector – shows the flow of logic\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI uses computer vision to interpret flowcharts.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Intro to Flowcharts"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c1c4ebd-88c9-4ddb-8061-b7a8095d9e6b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "382ac1bc-820e-4cd9-bc05-3e345c1d9c4a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Standardization matters not just for humans, but also because it makes it easier for AI to correctly understand and build working programs from a picture of a flowchart.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "382ac1bc-820e-4cd9-bc05-3e345c1d9c4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59b62aa5-68c5-4acb-b4a6-e847a88c0d23",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Create a Flowchart activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the Create a Flowchart activity guide. Click through the animated slide to show students an example of how to create a flowchart.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that test cases help test that the flowchart logic works. Make sure students try at least one value for each path of the if/else (e.g., one above and one below the temperature cutoff) and by tracing through the flowchart. If they are having trouble identifying that they need to set the temperature variable to different values, ask them what their condition or decision (yellow diamond) is checking."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59b62aa5-68c5-4acb-b4a6-e847a88c0d23",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd6512bb-d7ba-444e-bb19-e948995e5c3e",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd6512bb-d7ba-444e-bb19-e948995e5c3e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e3e73332-624b-4e7c-b144-ad0b2067879f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will use flowcharts to generate conditional logic and verify that their code correctly interprets user decisions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1. Students explore a thermometer app, then write code to change the conditional logic.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students can identify the main parts of the program: the variable, the event listener, and the if/else condition. Prompt with questions like: \"What value is the program checking?\" and \"What happens when the slider is moved?\" Support students in connecting the flowchart steps to the code.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Coding Conditionals with Flowcharts",
        "progression_name": "Explore: Temperature App",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This level is based on the flowchart that students completed in their Unit Guide. Direct students to trace through the flowchart if they are struggling to understand the code."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e3e73332-624b-4e7c-b144-ad0b2067879f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7117efd-06b5-4c72-90a6-ad20caf73e2d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7117efd-06b5-4c72-90a6-ad20caf73e2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2022b4dc-fa54-409e-8442-26d09f326ad5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What does the condition check for?*\n- *What did you change in the conditional logic?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that the condition is checking the value of the temperature variable which is controlled by the slider (or can be hard-coded by the programmer). Students should have made changes to the message text, message color, and background color. They may also have modified the condition, e.g. changed it to temperature > 80.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *conditional statement*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Boolean expression*.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students practice modeling decisions as flowcharts and translate flowchart decisions into JavaScript if/else statements using AI as a coding partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- Level 2: Students use Sketch Lab to build their own flowchart.\n- Level 3: Use AI to support them in generating code for conditional logic.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are including all key components including a start, process, decision, and outcome. Each flowchart must incorporate at least one variable and at least one function. Help students who are not sure of how to flowchart by asking prompting questions like: \"What decisions does the app have to make?\" or \"What should the user see on screen?\"",
        "progression_name": "Turning Flowcharts into Code"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2022b4dc-fa54-409e-8442-26d09f326ad5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64c73f07-16c3-4976-a7e4-5b8c232ba5ad",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are feeling confused by the flowchart elements, have them start with the yellow diamond decision point. Turn their decision into a question with (at least) two outcomes. Then have them work backwards to figure out any functions and variables needed for making that decision. Encourage students who finish early to incorporate more than one condition into their flowchart."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity as a formative check of how well students understand flowcharting conditional statements. Look for evidence that students are:\n- Able to use diamond shapes to represent a decision point\n- Flowcharting least two outcomes as a result of a decision point (e.g. Yes/No)"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64c73f07-16c3-4976-a7e4-5b8c232ba5ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "388f0adc-31ae-4ba9-985a-3495c37136f1",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "388f0adc-31ae-4ba9-985a-3495c37136f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bfb2af5d-a93f-403a-8fb3-0b714823194e",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Consider an if-else conditional you included in your code. How does your conditional connect to what the user sees or experiences?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that conditionals can change what is visible on screen, as well as controlling system behavior behind the scenes. This includes things like sound, data, or app state. Students should connect the decision being made in their code to both the user's experience (what they hear or see on screen) and the underlying changes happening in the program (like volume level or number of items in a cart)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bfb2af5d-a93f-403a-8fb3-0b714823194e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fb41d7b1-7fad-44b2-80fd-0bbb90353ac5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41ea9e59-eb4d-41ab-965f-af297127e31e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41ea9e59-eb4d-41ab-965f-af297127e31e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "56e37027-246f-4d02-accd-569687dc455d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16129a2f-5835-47e5-871b-8e533963854a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *When a customer clicks \"Add to Cart\" for an item of clothing, what information does the website need to know?*\n- *What if a piece of information, like size, is missing?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify the key pieces of information (e.g., size, color, quantity) needed for the system to complete the action and explain that missing information prevents the process from working correctly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16129a2f-5835-47e5-871b-8e533963854a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a84b66f1-e827-441c-bc56-5bb92ec0ac28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3838490b-e04b-45e4-bb2d-c681008f59e6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students will identify the key pieces of information needed to determine an outcome and recognize that the same process can be reused with different inputs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work with a partner to play the pizza guessing game. One student selects a pizza, and the other asks YES/NO questions to identify it. Students should switch roles after each round.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introduction to Functions with Parameters",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the game moving by limiting the number of questions or encouraging students to choose questions that narrow down options quickly."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3838490b-e04b-45e4-bb2d-c681008f59e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6fa4913a-273b-4144-b754-9d17e1270d19",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6fa4913a-273b-4144-b754-9d17e1270d19",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e7b4b14-2a1c-410d-90dc-04248b8c6c21",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which questions helped you narrow down the options fastest?*\n- *How could you reuse those same questions to find a different pizza?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify which questions most effectively narrow down the options and recognize that the same set of questions can be reused with different answers to produce different outcomes."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e7b4b14-2a1c-410d-90dc-04248b8c6c21",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95200ce0-618a-4907-9931-f645392d4d9a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In Guess the Pizza, you used the same set of questions to find different pizzas. Programmers do something similar — they write one set of instructions, called a function, that can produce different results depending on the information we give it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95200ce0-618a-4907-9931-f645392d4d9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19309bc8-b563-4f24-8fc0-098ac8c9da3b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out that the function (Order Pizza) stays the same, but the outputs change based on what information is passed in. Tell students they're about to learn what to call those pieces of information."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19309bc8-b563-4f24-8fc0-098ac8c9da3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54a2cc3d-dbc3-4412-b76d-89edb31ce43c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The questions you were asking are like categories (like \"topping\" or \"quantity\"), and the answers (like \"mushroom\" or \"2\") are the specific values. In programming, those categories become parameters, and the specific values are called arguments.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54a2cc3d-dbc3-4412-b76d-89edb31ce43c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea506632-3276-4e31-9179-9be8f4c7e161",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *parameter*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *argument*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1. Students explore a shopping app that doesn't use a function with parameters, then use AI to update it to use parameters.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Support students as they explore the app and work with AI to update the `addShirt` function. As you move around the room, ask students what information the function needs to know each time a shirt is added, and prompt them to distinguish what changes about the shirt each time from what stays the same. Check in on how they're describing what they want to the AI, asking what worked and what didn't, and encourage them to explain how they can tell whether the AI's updated function is actually doing what they asked.",
        "progression_name": "Exploring Functions",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are stuck on what to prompt the AI, encourage them to first identify the inputs the function needs (size, color, price, etc.) and describe those clearly before asking AI to update the code. Remind them to test the function with different values to verify it works as expected."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea506632-3276-4e31-9179-9be8f4c7e161",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f65fecc5-de65-42fc-9d35-ba27f3ab62fd",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f65fecc5-de65-42fc-9d35-ba27f3ab62fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "566df33a-0d75-46d2-8a79-ca96dc876414",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What information did you give the AI so it could update the addShirt function?*\n- *How did adding parameters change what the function could do?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that they needed to identify the specific pieces of information (like size, color, and price) before the AI could update the function effectively. They should also articulate that adding parameters made the function more flexible — one function can now handle many different shirts instead of being limited to a single hardcoded item."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "566df33a-0d75-46d2-8a79-ca96dc876414",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78484e1e-bad2-4334-8c37-fede5c2af71d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students will see how parameters are defined in a function and used in its body to produce flexible output, connecting the pizza activity's \"inputs that change the result\" idea to actual JavaScript syntax.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slides to show how an example of how to define a function and use a function’s parameters.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Planning and Implementing Functions",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect each step back to the Guess the Pizza activity. Emphasize that details like topping and quantity become parameters (inputs), and when the function is called, the specific values (like 3 or \"mushroom\") are the arguments. The function uses these inputs to update what appears on the screen."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78484e1e-bad2-4334-8c37-fede5c2af71d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b705b73-47f9-461f-9002-722169988388",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b705b73-47f9-461f-9002-722169988388",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f63a4b67-3918-4730-b80d-d96276ec77c4",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how the function is called when the button is clicked and point out how the arguments are connected to the parameters from the function definition. Point out how the values passed in become the inputs for the function.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that the values passed in (like 3 and \"mushroom\") are the arguments, which are the specific inputs used when the function runs, rather than the general categories (parameters)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f63a4b67-3918-4730-b80d-d96276ec77c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26419857-7319-4f2c-b012-c5841a393b54",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26419857-7319-4f2c-b012-c5841a393b54",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "079e10dc-d250-4b7c-8f6a-b3ac542f1674",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 and 3.\n- Level 2: Students plan and implement a function with parameters.\n- Level 3: Students complete the Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Support students as they plan and implement their own function with parameters on Level 2. As you move around the room, ask students which inputs their function needs and how those inputs will change the result on the page. Check that they can point to where the parameters appear in the function definition and where the function gets called with specific values. If students finish Level 2 quickly, prompt them to test their function with several different values to confirm the output changes as expected.",
        "progression_name": "Implementing Functions with Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "079e10dc-d250-4b7c-8f6a-b3ac542f1674",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2de9d97-da66-472d-9a1d-3e8286ca13e4",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2de9d97-da66-472d-9a1d-3e8286ca13e4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3531ff01-c9bb-4671-9b96-667b323f7bce"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87dc3407-cdbe-457c-8ef5-e1a5af2241dd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts:\n- *How did parameters make your function more flexible?*\n- *Where did AI help or confuse you in the process?*",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Normalize the idea that AI is a tool and not always perfect. Emphasize the importance of critical thinking and verifying AI-generated output. Discuss how AI can be a powerful learning aid when used effectively."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87dc3407-cdbe-457c-8ef5-e1a5af2241dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d51cb3a-dd12-4f4c-b8c0-a19fb0c5ee75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b71c1c1e-22de-4ae3-94e3-2b565d4738f2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b71c1c1e-22de-4ae3-94e3-2b565d4738f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d51cb3a-dd12-4f4c-b8c0-a19fb0c5ee75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3111a1cd-fb83-4ecc-a93f-ae3b41cd28f5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3111a1cd-fb83-4ecc-a93f-ae3b41cd28f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d51cb3a-dd12-4f4c-b8c0-a19fb0c5ee75"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f68cbf3c-1a65-4f72-8b95-da33bddde89f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze how interactive elements on a webpage support usability using the POUR principles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how the POUR principles apply to interactivity on a web page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What is changing on the page?*\n- *When does it change?*\n- *Which part of POUR does it support? How?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify what changes on a dynamic page, when it changes, and connect those changes to POUR:\n- The password feedback messages (errors and \"strong password\") and their colors update.\n- As the user types in the password field (in real time).\n- Perceivable → Users can see clear visual feedback (colors/messages); Understandable → Messages explain what is wrong or correct.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f68cbf3c-1a65-4f72-8b95-da33bddde89f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f818420a-1655-4915-9f2e-bd4ec8dcc299"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1fae7a42-1b39-404d-a182-988d9b11100c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students identify how interactive features change a page and begin organizing that behavior using functions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 1, Level 1. Students explore different dynamic, interactive features and see how they change a page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Level 1, guide students to notice: the event (click, input, etc.), the code that runs when the event happens, and the specific lines that update the page. If students are stuck, prompt: \"Find the line that changes something on the page. What is it doing?\"",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Explore Dynamic Features",
        "progression_name": "Explore Dynamic Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1fae7a42-1b39-404d-a182-988d9b11100c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6528bc7-0e94-469a-b659-1baf2b5eafe9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aadbcf0e-b33f-4ed5-aa06-40da464922d8",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to introduce the core components of a dynamic web page (event, state, logic, update) and ensure students understand what each one means.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to introduce the example and have students identify each part (event, state, logic, update). Ask them to connect each part to what is happening in the app."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aadbcf0e-b33f-4ed5-aa06-40da464922d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6528bc7-0e94-469a-b659-1baf2b5eafe9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24a1a559-2518-4e2e-a735-fc62a762ab0a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students plan, build, and evaluate a dynamic feature using functions, logic, and user interaction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 4, Level 2 through 4.\n- Level 2: Brainstorm a dynamic feature with AI Chat.\n- Level 3: Plan, build, label, evaluate, and improve the feature.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check for Understanding.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Plan and Generate Dynamic Features",
        "progression_name": "Generate Dynamic Features"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24a1a559-2518-4e2e-a735-fc62a762ab0a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6528bc7-0e94-469a-b659-1baf2b5eafe9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36d560eb-cfa3-4af1-8b29-7438d89fd22c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Push students to move beyond \"it works\" to understanding how it works. As they build, have them pause and point to where the interaction starts (event), what information is being tracked (variables/state), and which lines actually update the page."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 to assess whether students can apply variables and parameters to make dynamic changes to the DOM and revise interactions to improve usability. Look for evidence that students use variables to control what updates on the page, pass values through functions or parameters, and make intentional changes that improve clarity and user experience (e.g., clearer feedback, more visible updates)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36d560eb-cfa3-4af1-8b29-7438d89fd22c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6528bc7-0e94-469a-b659-1baf2b5eafe9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1f03cb6-8c4a-45b3-a860-37328c96db05",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1f03cb6-8c4a-45b3-a860-37328c96db05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "897cb38f-da38-4924-abfe-2a1f1c2ab8a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2830d1e-0cff-4a18-b15b-39da37f7d909",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Instruct students to follow the paths on the flowchart that apply to them and complete the corresponding task, then have students explain why they completed that task.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When might a web app need to make different decisions for different users?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students understand that real-world apps use nested conditionals to personalize experiences and handle different user paths. For example:\n- Age gating: Showing different content if a user is under 13 or over 18\n- Login status: Showing a dashboard if you're logged in, or a sign-up page if you're not\n- Recommendations: Suggesting videos, products, or music based on past behavior\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2830d1e-0cff-4a18-b15b-39da37f7d909",
        "lesson_activity.key": "456fe5bb-2199-4edb-9127-15232f34397b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea16792b-1b3b-4a3e-8aed-c516e572a1ac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students build foundational understanding of nested if/else statements by translating a simple flowchart into working code. They’ll learn how each decision branch connects to a UI change, define what nested conditionals are, and use AI support to debug and confirm that all paths behave as expected.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define nested conditionals. Emphasize that a nested conditional is just a conditional inside another conditional, where one question leads to another question.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Flowchart to Code",
        "progression_name": "Movie Spoiler App"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea16792b-1b3b-4a3e-8aed-c516e572a1ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbcb3680-61a2-4bce-8a52-9589414ab545",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just looked at a nested conditional in the warm up. If one condition is true, you continue down the path to the next condition. In this example, we first ask if a student is wearing sneakers. Then, depending on their answer, we ask a new question like \"Wearing glasses?\" or \"Wearing blue?\" Similarly, AI uses a branching, tree-like structure to model decisions and their possible consequences. So when we build nested conditionals in code, we’re actually modeling the same kind of thinking AI systems use to classify things.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Have students trace through each path individually. See if they can come up with additional questions to ask that could lead to new outcomes. For example, instead of \"Wearing blue?\" you could ask \"Wearing boots?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbcb3680-61a2-4bce-8a52-9589414ab545",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3688134c-3873-4ec5-9b9d-8eab42322e9a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3688134c-3873-4ec5-9b9d-8eab42322e9a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95405c81-2f98-4e9a-85d4-6e47fe14b436",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8 Level 1. Students will explore the code and app to complete the notes.md file.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students test out the app's if/else logic, have them trace through the code to see what they understand. Ask them to explain how each path changes the UI and how they're using all four test cases (True/True, True/ False, False/True, False/False) to verify correctness.",
        "progression_name": "Explore Nested Conditionals"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95405c81-2f98-4e9a-85d4-6e47fe14b436",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cbd6f76c-4f53-447b-af64-a762dc786da9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why does the app need to check both conditions instead of just one?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should understand that having multiple conditions can affect the program’s outcome. Checking each condition adds new possible paths (combinations of true/false), in this case leading to four different outcomes. Whereas checking only one condition would lead to incorrect or incomplete behavior."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cbd6f76c-4f53-447b-af64-a762dc786da9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93780ca2-8bde-4701-b773-3d743289cec5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students apply their understanding of nested if/else logic by planning, coding, and testing a multi-decision feature. They’ll use AI to turn their flowchart into working code, debug and refine AI's logic, and verify that all four paths behave as intended through systematic testing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to recall how a conditional statement works.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain how a nested conditional flowchart is represented in code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *A hiring system uses this rule. Who's excluded?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students name specific people excluded by this rule — a self-taught developer with 20 years of experience, a career-switcher with 4 years and a CS degree, a returning parent. Because both branches must be TRUE, anyone missing either signal is filtered out — even when they're clearly qualified. Students recognize that the code looks neutral but the rule is not: choosing yearsOfExperience and hasCSDegree as the gates is a value decision, not just a technical one.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- Level 2: Students will plan a feature by creating a flowchart with nested decisions and test cases.\n- Level 3: Students will implement their plan in code, debug using test cases, and refine with AI support.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students notice how AI can make implementing nested conditionals faster and easier but if their logic is confusing, the AI’s output may be wrong. As students implement their nested if/else logic, check that their code directly maps to the flowchart and ensure they are testing all four outcomes.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introduction to Nested Conditionals",
        "progression_name": "Plan and Build Your App Logic"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93780ca2-8bde-4701-b773-3d743289cec5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "122baadc-4e1f-43dd-b058-1f2521771223",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "122baadc-4e1f-43dd-b058-1f2521771223",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80003ce6-9db6-41b3-8ce6-c841704e97c9",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80003ce6-9db6-41b3-8ce6-c841704e97c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5b9b8ce-ec7c-4b2b-b746-ae91dd88ac8b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How did you check AI's work?*\n- *Did AI miss any paths in your flowchart?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should discuss that they tested all four possible outcomes and matched what they saw on screen to their flowchart. Students may notice that the AI assumed one condition mattered more (e.g., finished movie = true) — a small example of bias in logic or oversimplification.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Prompt students to connect this debugging step to real-world examples of biased or incomplete algorithms (e.g., recommendation systems showing only one kind of result)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5b9b8ce-ec7c-4b2b-b746-ae91dd88ac8b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d37941bf-8c39-40b6-b570-0e6a5a57c375"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41fcf392-9409-4249-9e75-a266fabca308",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Name a real app whose rules you'd want to read. Who would you need to ask to see them?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students name specific apps (TikTok, Instagram, Spotify, YouTube, college admissions, credit-score systems, hiring tools) and recognize that the rules behind each are hidden by default. They articulate that the \"who would you ask\" answer depends on accountability — the engineer, the company, a regulator, a journalist, or the affected community. The point is that the rules exist, someone wrote them, and someone is responsible for them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41fcf392-9409-4249-9e75-a266fabca308",
        "lesson_activity.key": "141bc852-7894-47e3-be59-bbca6ebdf62a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1848f1fb-6e7a-4379-9df6-ddd07c55bd6a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the example style rules, then have students come up with their own rule, using either 'and' or 'or' and discuss it with a partner."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1848f1fb-6e7a-4379-9df6-ddd07c55bd6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bc7fd2f-1679-4baf-b120-cf5cbc1e201d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we're going to explore how combining conditional statements using AND and OR can make our programs more robust. Real web apps often need to check more than one condition at a time. Simple if-else statements can only handle one test, but combining conditions helps your code make smarter decisions — like showing confidential information only if a user is logged in AND verified.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bc7fd2f-1679-4baf-b120-cf5cbc1e201d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf044fd9-785a-4362-ad37-0c64d8f5e709",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf044fd9-785a-4362-ad37-0c64d8f5e709",
        "lesson_activity.key": "405289d2-7562-4879-a8a5-53d250e0a1bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6865378-3766-4944-824f-b28006df67ed",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will be able to explore web pages that use combined conditional statements with && (AND) and || (OR).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Combining Conditions with AND/OR activity guide and the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1. Have students work in pairs to predict what message will appear for each combination of checkboxes before testing. Then have them toggle the checkboxes to test their predictions and record patterns they notice in their activity guide. Avoid explaining how AND and OR work at this stage—focus on observation and pattern-finding.\n- Level 1: Students test how different checkbox combinations affect the result and record what they notice for all four cases: TT, TF, FT, FF.\n- Level 2: Students repeat the process with a different rule and compare how the results change across the same four cases.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Exploring Conditions",
        "progression_name": "Using AND and OR",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to make predictions before testing. If needed, model how to toggle the checkboxes, but do not explain how the logic works yet. If students ask for the rule, redirect with: \"What are you noticing so far?\" or \"Try another combination and see what happens.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6865378-3766-4944-824f-b28006df67ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0bef0684-b2ac-4f25-8282-6a85a53a0936",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0bef0684-b2ac-4f25-8282-6a85a53a0936",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "066edbf6-e925-44be-ad09-f305d125ffe7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What patterns did you notice in Levels 1 and 2 when the result turned true or false?*\n- *How were the results in Level 2 different from Level 1?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize patterns in how results change across combinations of inputs and begin comparing those patterns between the two levels. Look for students to notice that in one case the result is only true when both conditions are true, while in the other case the result is true when at least one condition is true. Students may describe this in their own words (e.g., \"both have to be on\" vs \"just one works\"), which sets up the transition to formal terminology."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "066edbf6-e925-44be-ad09-f305d125ffe7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6b7cb2d-4f01-4d94-858a-f4a0186831ab",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've been testing different combinations and noticing patterns in when the result is true or false. These patterns actually have names in programming. When a result is only true if both conditions are true, we call that AND. When a result is true if at least one condition is true, we call that OR.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6b7cb2d-4f01-4d94-858a-f4a0186831ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58e0a832-4bbe-4732-bbcf-71c147107494",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain AND (&&) by building on the patterns students just discussed. Ask students to restate when the result was true in Level 1, and guide them to recognize that both conditions needed to be true. Name this pattern as AND and connect it to the table, pointing out that only the case where both values are true results in a true outcome. Also clarify that AND combines two conditions (like the two checkboxes) to produce one result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain OR (||) by building on the patterns students identified in Level 2. Ask students to describe when the result was true, and guide them to recognize that the result was true when at least one condition was true. Name this pattern as OR and connect it to the table, pointing out that all cases except when both values are false result in a true outcome. Also clarify that OR combines two conditions (like the two checkboxes) to produce one result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that each row in the table becomes one path in the flowchart. Walk through one case step-by-step (e.g., both conditions are true), pointing to the flowchart as you trace the path to an outcome. Then briefly show how the other cases would follow different paths. Make sure to explain that when \"Has Ticket\" is false, we don't need to check the second condition when working with AND—both remaining cases (False/True and False/False) lead to No Access.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, pause after modeling one path and ask: \"Where would a different case go?\" or \"Which row from the table does this path represent?\" Have students point or describe before you confirm."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58e0a832-4bbe-4732-bbcf-71c147107494",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95bb0890-e89e-4ce6-b889-b6cbdd1954bc",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will use a truth table to design a flowchart that represents all possible outcomes of two conditions, then translate that logic into code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 5.\n- Level 3: Students create a flowchart for their chosen scenario.\n- Level 4: Students convert their flowchart into code and test it for all 4 possible outcomes.\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check for Understanding.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Coding a Multi-Condition Flowchart ",
        "progression_name": "Create and Implement a Flowchart"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95bb0890-e89e-4ce6-b889-b6cbdd1954bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c65051fd-cd04-476d-a930-f82677f6e5ea",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to check that their flowchart includes all four possible cases. If students get stuck, prompt them to refer back to their tables and ask: \"Which case is this representing?\""
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 3 and 4 to assess whether students can accurately represent and apply multi-condition logic. Look for evidence that students:\n- Design a flowchart where each outcome matches a row from their truth table\n- Ensure their flowchart includes all four possible cases\n- Translate their flowchart into code that produces the correct result for each case"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c65051fd-cd04-476d-a930-f82677f6e5ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c0053cc0-33b9-46f3-95e9-a978de684b17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ba6b7e9-3eff-48b0-9d61-18ac20bda00c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how an app can use multiple conditions together to make decisions, using examples from the lesson (such as checkboxes, AND/OR logic, or flowcharts). After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. As students share, highlight responses that mention combining conditions and checking multiple possibilities (e.g., using AND or OR) to determine an outcome."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ba6b7e9-3eff-48b0-9d61-18ac20bda00c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1df1a9ab-dab4-41e4-8499-f8e04a7b1ea0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5144eec-0d13-4f3c-872f-07e461cd2d97",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *You apply for a student discount but the AI says \"Not eligible\". Why might this happen?*\n- *What rule might the AI be using?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The goal of this discussion is for students to begin recognizing that AI decisions are based on underlying rules or conditions rather than independent reasoning. Students should start to infer that these rules often rely on thresholds (such as income limits) or required information, and that unclear or incomplete rules can lead to outcomes that feel confusing or unfair. Listen for students suggesting possible conditions the AI might be using and beginning to connect decisions to specific criteria rather than vague ideas.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to move beyond surface-level responses, prompt them to think about what information the AI might need to make a decision and what could be missing or misinterpreted. You can introduce a simple example like an income cutoff to help ground their thinking, but avoid fully defining the rule for them so they continue to generate their own ideas."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5144eec-0d13-4f3c-872f-07e461cd2d97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "676a8f12-7bda-4836-a98e-beddb2d0306b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab976db2-7e22-463e-abb8-c21b84bdea1e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab976db2-7e22-463e-abb8-c21b84bdea1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "676a8f12-7bda-4836-a98e-beddb2d0306b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "333df241-230a-4f05-a94a-2ad62051ae1e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "333df241-230a-4f05-a94a-2ad62051ae1e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "676a8f12-7bda-4836-a98e-beddb2d0306b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4ce8b3f-4492-4a1b-b8f5-29ff5183a730",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students recognize that a value changes in response to user actions and controls what appears on the screen. This sets up the NOT operator as a way to represent that change and prepares students to read more complex conditions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI and Algorithmic Decisions Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1. Have them click the toggle multiple times and observe what changes, paying attention to both the visual changes and the value on the screen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students notice both the visual changes and the `isOn` value. Some may focus only on the appearance and miss the value changing. Prompt them to be specific by asking what exactly is changing each time they click. Encourage students to click multiple times to see the pattern.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "The NOT Operator",
        "progression_name": "Exploring the NOT Operator"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4ce8b3f-4492-4a1b-b8f5-29ff5183a730",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5a6734d-bead-46a2-941b-d4dc00ca3f19",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5a6734d-bead-46a2-941b-d4dc00ca3f19",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ed21b23-e838-4a21-8441-8b0437b14cfb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide the display the prompts.\n- *Where have you seen something switch ON/OFF?*\n- *What changed each time you clicked? What stayed the same?*\n- *What value was changing?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** The goal of this discussion is for students to connect the idea of a value switching between two states to familiar examples in everyday technology. Students should recognize that many apps use this kind of logic, even if they don’t see the code behind it. Listen for students identifying examples like mute/unmute, password visibility, or dark mode, and help them connect those examples back to the idea of a value flipping between true and false. Students should also articulate that a value in the program is changing each time they click, and that this change controls what they see on the screen. Students should move beyond general observations like \"it switches\" and instead identify that the isOn value changes from true to false and drives the visual updates. Listen for students connecting the visible changes to this underlying value.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give vague responses, ask follow-up questions like \"What does the value say before and after you click?\" or \"What exactly is changing in the program?\" to push them toward more precise explanations."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ed21b23-e838-4a21-8441-8b0437b14cfb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f29abd28-c6bc-4287-a0c7-db19fa6246bc",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f29abd28-c6bc-4287-a0c7-db19fa6246bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53c68dea-e8b4-4da8-ac33-ef2517bf252e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "So we've seen that this value flips each time you click — from false to true and back again. In code, we have a way to represent that exact change. It's called the NOT operator.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53c68dea-e8b4-4da8-ac33-ef2517bf252e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eab3ca08-e081-485a-8164-fe719dd43934",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the NOT operator. Connect directly to the previous discussion by naming what students observed. Explain that when they clicked the toggle, the value changed from false to true and back again, and that in code, this change is represented using the NOT (!) operator. Emphasize that NOT describes the behavior they just observed—flipping a value from true to false or false to true.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the explanation concise and focused on the idea of \"flipping a value.\" Let the visual table reinforce the pattern and use student observations from the previous discussion to anchor understanding."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eab3ca08-e081-485a-8164-fe719dd43934",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "198f286d-710f-4f3d-8bbf-e2b8c587e264",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "198f286d-710f-4f3d-8bbf-e2b8c587e264",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4663065-c8b6-4067-af7b-0b91e2a57f18",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students first predict what !isOn will be in each case. Then walk through the example and explicitly point out how the NOT (!) operator is used in front of a value. Show that adding ! changes the value from true to false or false to true. Connect this directly to the idea that NOT flips the value, and now we are seeing how that behavior is written in code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the condition and give students time to think before discussing. Ask them to identify all the different ways the condition could be true. Guide them to break the expression into parts, noticing that some values must work together (like isStudent && hasID), while others can make the condition true on their own (like hasSpecialPass).",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, prompt them to focus on one part of the condition at a time and describe what would happen if only that part were true."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4663065-c8b6-4067-af7b-0b91e2a57f18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbaa1d97-75b2-4c2a-a107-75390a773f55",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbaa1d97-75b2-4c2a-a107-75390a773f55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87b83f89-8450-448f-8a7d-f30cfd1e9234",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Every rule you write today gets enforced by code — and every user who runs into that rule and doesn't understand it ends up talking to Customer Support. They're the human face of the rules engineers and product managers designed. They're also the ones who can tell you that your rule is wrong, because they hear it 50 times a day.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87b83f89-8450-448f-8a7d-f30cfd1e9234",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4894aed3-2aee-4df7-b9a7-4f3eaa8f22af",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the role of a Customer Support Specialist."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4894aed3-2aee-4df7-b9a7-4f3eaa8f22af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d68bccba-36c6-4e48-a2a9-45498afc7f2a",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students move from writing logic to understanding and evaluating it. They analyze existing code, use AI to explain and critique logic, refactor nested conditionals into combined Boolean expressions, and consider how rules impact different users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 5.\n- Level 2: Students interview a Customer Support Specialist chatbot.\n- Level 3: Students investigate and fix a logic issue.\n- Level 4: Students analyze provided code, use AI to explain and evaluate the logic, and refactor it into a combined Boolean expression.\n- Level 5: Students complete the Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are actually reading and reasoning about the code before using AI. Some students may rely too quickly on AI explanations without verifying them. Prompt students to explain the logic in their own words and check whether the AI explanation matches what the code is doing. As students refactor, check that they are preserving the original logic while rewriting it using !, &&, and ||. In Level 4, listen for whether students can clearly describe when the rule is true and who might be affected by it.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Work with ! and Complex Conditions",
        "progression_name": "Work with ! and Complex Conditions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d68bccba-36c6-4e48-a2a9-45498afc7f2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f13f07b-8814-4f9f-8582-c900de65ce37",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to treat AI as a thinking partner rather than a solution generator. Have them ask AI to explain the logic, then compare that explanation to their own understanding before making changes. Remind students that their goal is not just to get working code, but to understand how the logic works and how it can be improved."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 2, 3 and 4 to assess whether students can interpret and refactor complex conditions using !, &&, and ||, and explain when a rule is true and how it may impact different users."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f13f07b-8814-4f9f-8582-c900de65ce37",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2da74f88-4a44-44f3-a6c6-a001ff5b558d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6c0103c-f88a-4dd1-a8dc-dfb8499512fb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how they can design and debug more complex rules by breaking conditions into parts, testing different cases, and checking how the logic behaves. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class. As students share, highlight responses that mention breaking down conditions, checking when a rule is true, and evaluating how logic affects different users."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6c0103c-f88a-4dd1-a8dc-dfb8499512fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988d6b5c-9198-4a2e-99f7-224be3e8f64e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "837d709a-e5a4-4585-841a-5edcd5301983",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s an example of an app, game, or website that reacts in a way you really like ? Why do you think it feels satisfying or fun?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify qualities of engaging interactivity to inspire their own app designs and ground today’s planning in real user experiences.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "837d709a-e5a4-4585-841a-5edcd5301983",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "474b6a12-a5a5-46d2-a4a8-63866ed0f078",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the first part of the project requirements.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Project Rubric.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Reactive Logic Project is divided into four milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from defining their concept to showcasing their final product.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "474b6a12-a5a5-46d2-a4a8-63866ed0f078",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5b3aeb8-7c92-45b6-bd93-f71624a705c4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5b3aeb8-7c92-45b6-bd93-f71624a705c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "33614ba6-634a-4b99-bb80-6063ba85dc2b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students plan their app idea.\n- Level 2: Students create a wireframe to plan the app layout.\n- Level 3: Students create a flowchart to illustrate the logic of their app.\n- Level 4: Students complete the Milestone 1 Reflection.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students explore each level, ask guiding questions such as:\n- \"What is the main interaction in your app?\"\n- \"Where does the user interact with your app, and what happens in response?\"\n- \"How does your flowchart represent what your app should do?\"",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "33614ba6-634a-4b99-bb80-6063ba85dc2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7976ad4-c9b4-43a3-a96b-e2e0ea87d1b4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to move quickly from idea to design to logic. These levels are meant to help them get a clear starting point—not to fully plan every detail."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7976ad4-c9b4-43a3-a96b-e2e0ea87d1b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96d198f9-e62a-454d-94b2-683e720d608f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96d198f9-e62a-454d-94b2-683e720d608f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69b48c23-9a73-425d-beec-0eaedd6fa599",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Thinking back to the app you mentioned in the warm-up that reacts in a satisfying way — does your planned app have any of those same qualities? What would you need to add to make it feel that way?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect their own design choices to the qualities of engaging interactivity they identified earlier, helping them notice gaps between their current plan and the kind of user experience they actually want to create.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1 Reflection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69b48c23-9a73-425d-beec-0eaedd6fa599",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6fcf9bc2-3cde-4870-aab4-e12c5b315dd5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bafeeda5-d6ce-4722-8d28-1e2bfe595fe7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one part of your app that you're most excited to see working? What's one piece you're not sure how to build yet?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate clear goals and challenges for their first build session, helping them prioritize tasks and seek targeted support.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bafeeda5-d6ce-4722-8d28-1e2bfe595fe7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01a86911-8e22-4e45-b50c-a78252320303",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 and 6.\n- Level 5: Students start working on their Reactive Logic Project.\n- Level 6: Students complete the Milestone 2 Reflection.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a working first interaction. Ask check-in questions like:\n- \"What is the first interaction you're building?\"\n- \"What should happen when the user interacts with your app?\"\n- \"How will you know your interaction is working correctly?\"",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01a86911-8e22-4e45-b50c-a78252320303",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b9f6c30-1f5a-4480-9709-3273c5cb71ab",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2 Reflection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b9f6c30-1f5a-4480-9709-3273c5cb71ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6bae210-dd8a-4270-9772-2b520a2c6637",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What was one moment today where the AI gave you something unexpected? What did you decide to do about it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should describe a specific moment of AI output that surprised, disappointed, or confused them — not a general feeling that \"the AI didn't get it right,\" but a concrete example of what the AI produced and why it didn't match their intent. For the second part, students should articulate a deliberate decision: they either refined their prompt and tried again, accepted the output and explained why it actually worked, or manually adjusted something and explained what they changed. Push students who say they just accepted the AI's output without reflection — ask them whether the result actually matched their user and their design intention from Day 1.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6bae210-dd8a-4270-9772-2b520a2c6637",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa478d88-f0d4-45af-9e1b-785af3f759a6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9d07fb5-3317-4ae1-b183-e8063de79896",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one bug, glitch, or weird behavior your app is showing right now?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice describing and analyzing problems out loud, normalizing debugging as part of the creative process and preparing for peer or AI-assisted troubleshooting.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9d07fb5-3317-4ae1-b183-e8063de79896",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4579f2b8-8633-4cca-abac-4c0b109b5179",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Peer Feedback with TAG handout.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up and exchange apps. Students should review one to two peers’ projects and use the TAG method (Tell, Ask, Give) to provide feedback using the provided handout. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it’s an opportunity to gather feedback and identify ways to improve their app.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product. Reinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance. If time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4579f2b8-8633-4cca-abac-4c0b109b5179",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5fd70c7e-9fde-48cf-b224-5eb671e1c062",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5fd70c7e-9fde-48cf-b224-5eb671e1c062",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a89421e0-7ea0-4bbb-a5b7-1939119e1b51",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 7 to complete Levels 7 through 9.\n- Level 7: Students continue working on their app.\n- Level 8: Students use the Peer Feedback with TAG handout to get peer feedback.\n- Level 9: Students complete the Milestone 3 Reflection.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask guiding questions such as:\n- \"Are all your planned conditions working as expected?\"\n- \"What happens when you try different inputs?\"\n- \"How are you using functions and variables to control your app's behavior?\"",
        "name": "Milestone 3",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a89421e0-7ea0-4bbb-a5b7-1939119e1b51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d06cd05-ea9e-4a89-8307-ff2f8f0ee45b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3 Reflection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d06cd05-ea9e-4a89-8307-ff2f8f0ee45b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16594286-5e31-43f1-983c-b7733b3ff92e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Did watching someone else use your app reveal anything you couldn't see on your own? What did they notice that you missed?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that users experience apps differently than creators do — the designer knows what's supposed to happen, but the user only sees what actually happens. This builds the habit of testing with real people instead of just testing in your own head.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3 Reflection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16594286-5e31-43f1-983c-b7733b3ff92e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ccf3baea-7266-4176-a45d-e848e0f8d155"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9b3bba81-a8ca-427e-8e8b-5f639cd66b4a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If someone were testing your app right now, what’s the one thing you’d want them to notice first?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the purpose and user experience of their app, setting an intentional focus for peer testing and feedback during today’s class.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9b3bba81-a8ca-427e-8e8b-5f639cd66b4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d83eee5e-016c-422f-9296-190fd90cfa4a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 10 to work on their project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 4.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product. Reinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance. If time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d83eee5e-016c-422f-9296-190fd90cfa4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d0e9707-160a-4efb-8c99-34ea673f1d79",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d0e9707-160a-4efb-8c99-34ea673f1d79",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5577499-b9af-43a3-94fb-e266107863fe",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Looking at your app right now, what's the one thing that has to work well tomorrow for you to feel proud of it? What are you willing to leave imperfect?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice making the kind of tradeoff decisions real designers make before a release — identifying the highest-impact fix and consciously letting go of lower-priority items.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5577499-b9af-43a3-94fb-e266107863fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "76bdbe1a-3cf7-4431-9576-e2bb7d5cd5d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90a3bb46-236c-4ee9-88c0-e7621543873a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one thing you still need to check or polish before your app feels ready to share?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students self-assess their progress and identify final next steps, using available resources and the rubric to guide productive final work time.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90a3bb46-236c-4ee9-88c0-e7621543873a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6277ec54-655c-4104-abd9-d6ea032e566a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the Project Rubric. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\" If time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6277ec54-655c-4104-abd9-d6ea032e566a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "095c6347-e5a2-4832-9b12-73af88bcfb6d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "095c6347-e5a2-4832-9b12-73af88bcfb6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cfdf282-f8ef-4767-b19e-0b0c1c87daf3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 10 to complete Levels 10 and 11.\n- Level 10: Students finalize their project.\n- Level 11: Students complete the final project reflection.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task. Remind students to comment their code with meaningful descriptions. Have students review their rubric and prioritize work on any rows where they do not yet show extensive evidence.",
        "progression_name": "Final Project Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional designers always prioritize fixes before a release. Say something like: \"You don't need to fix everything today — focus on what will have the biggest impact for your user. Iteration is about making smart choices with the time you have.\" If time allows at the end, invite students to do a brief pair preview, where they open a peer's site and give one final piece of positive or constructive feedback."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cfdf282-f8ef-4767-b19e-0b0c1c87daf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97c6ef0a-eb3f-4b3f-a874-3ae27ddf0e82",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97c6ef0a-eb3f-4b3f-a874-3ae27ddf0e82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc062f16-d3b0-4bf7-baff-ae8d531d0bda",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their Reactive Logic Apps on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage students to leave a note about what they find interesting or insightful about their peers' work. Ensure that students are thoughtfully engaging with a variety of projects, not just those of their closest peers.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc062f16-d3b0-4bf7-baff-ae8d531d0bda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a866bef0-661a-4672-9a0d-9b18ab31cf91",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a866bef0-661a-4672-9a0d-9b18ab31cf91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fb787925-c33b-4c45-871b-47f9900af207",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When was AI most helpful to you? When did you need to rely on your own judgment instead?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on AI as a collaborator with strengths and limits, articulating where it accelerated their work and where their own decisions mattered more.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fb787925-c33b-4c45-871b-47f9900af207",
        "lesson_activity.key": "852c9cb3-d50a-4faf-a852-643a72231b35"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e3ecb35-db6d-4a65-ae41-e03690cd3598",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Unit Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
